mptcp-bpf-weight for Debian (server/VPS side)
===============================================

This is the server/VPS counterpart of the router's OpenWrt
mptcp-bpf-weight and mptcp-weight-manager packages built by the same
openmptcprouter-feeds source tree (see that repo's ARCHITECTURE.md for
the router-vs-VPS split, and mptcp-bpf-dscp's identical README for the
analogous DSCP-scheduler split this one mirrors).

On the router, each WAN has its own local IP, so weights are set per WAN
via that IP (endpoint_weights map). On this VPS, every subflow arrives
on the same local IP, so weight by MPTCP remote endpoint id instead
(weight_remote_id map, shared with mptcp-bpf-weight-rr):

    mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh set id <N> <weight>

<N> is the MPTCP endpoint id the router assigned to the WAN that should
carry that weight. On the router that id is network.<iface>.ip4table,
kept stable across reconnects (DHCP renewal, mobile reconnects) once the
router's mptcp/multipath script passes it explicitly to
'ip mptcp endpoint add ... id <N>' instead of letting the kernel
auto-assign one. Look it up on the router with:

    uci get network.<iface>.ip4table

The router's openmptcprouter-vps init script syncs this automatically:
whenever network.<iface>.multipath_weight changes, its
040-multipath-weight tracker hook calls set_mptcp_weight_vps, which
POSTs the current weights to this VPS's /mptcp_weight API route (see
omradmin.py) -- no manual copying needed, mirroring how DSCP's
dscp_remote_id sync works via /mptcp_dscp.

    mptcp-scheduler-weight.sh show

lists every currently configured weight, both by interface/IP and by
remote id.

Which scheduler
----------------

bpf_weight always sends on the single highest-weight active subflow
(only one WAN carries traffic for a given weight tier at a time). For
the weighted-round-robin alternative that instead distributes send
bursts across ALL active subflows proportionally to weight (every
active WAN still carries some traffic, just not equally), install
mptcp-bpf-weight-rr - a separate source/binary package, see its own
debian/README.Debian. Install whichever matches the router's
network.globals.mptcp_scheduler setting; installing both is harmless,
but only enable (systemctl enable) the one matching that setting, since
both ultimately write the same net.mptcp.scheduler sysctl. Either way,
this package's mptcp-weight-manager is what configures weights - it
works against both schedulers' shared BPF maps.

Service
-------

mptcp-bpf-weight.service loads /usr/share/bpf/scheduler/mptcp_bpf_weight.o,
pins it (and its maps) under /sys/fs/bpf/mptcp, and sets it as
net.mptcp.scheduler at boot. This changes the *system-wide* default
MPTCP scheduler on this host - only install/enable this (or
mptcp-bpf-weight-rr, not both) on a VPS dedicated to terminating
OpenMPTCProuter multipath connections.

After building/installing a new .o (e.g. after editing the scheduler),
run:

    systemctl restart mptcp-bpf-weight

Stopping the service switches net.mptcp.scheduler back to "default"
before unpinning, so it doesn't leave the sysctl pointed at a scheduler
name the kernel no longer has registered.

Requirements
------------

A kernel with MPTCP struct_ops scheduler support (BPF_STRUCT_OPS for
mptcp_sched_ops), CONFIG_BPF, CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF and CONFIG_MPTCP
enabled. This is a fairly recent kernel feature - check
'bpftool struct_ops register' actually succeeds if the service fails to
start (see 'systemctl status mptcp-bpf-weight').
